{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["He said concerning forbidden relations: <b>Speak to the children of Israel</b> alone, because the warning against forbidden relations is identical for all Israel — the priests are not singled out in it — and therefore He grouped them all together as one. And He said at the beginning \"I am YHWH your Elohim,\" for the same reason as in the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:2). And the meaning of \"Speak to the children of Israel and say to them, I am YHWH your Elohim\" is as though He said \"and you shall say to them in My name, I am YHWH your Elohim.\" And likewise \"You shall be holy, for I, YHWH your Elohim, am holy\" (Leviticus 19:2). And it is possible that the reason is that when Moses went out from before HaShem without a veil and went out and spoke to the children of Israel that which he was commanded, he did not need to say to them \"Thus says YHWH,\" for it was known to them that His spirit would speak in him and His word upon his tongue — he would not speak on his own. And so in many places in Mishneh Torah he speaks thus, such as \"And it shall be if you will indeed obey My commandments which I command you this day, then I will give the rain of your land and I will give grass in your field\" (Deuteronomy 11:13–15) — and it is not Moses who gives rain upon the face of the earth and sends water upon the streets and causes grass to grow in the field, but rather HaShem blessed be He. And likewise \"And HaShem has not given you a heart to know, and I led you in the wilderness, bread you did not eat and wine and strong drink you did not drink, in order that you may know that I am YHWH your Elohim\" (Deuteronomy 29:3–5). And the meaning of \"to love HaShem your Elohim\" has already been explained (Exodus 24:1)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:04.083547861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}